22:27:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s a bit of an exaggeration to say “everyone” thought the Internet was a fad, but there was certainly a loud chorus of skepticism in the early-to-mid 1990s. This doubt came from several overlapping reasons: **1. It looked like a niche hobby for academics and techies** Before the World Wide Web became user-friendly (around 1993–94 with Mosaic and later Netscape), the Internet was mostly text-based email, Usenet newsgroups, and file transfers. It seemed arcane, command-line driven, and utterly inaccessible to the average person—like ham radio or early personal computers that only enthusiasts used. **2. It was slow, ugly, and hard to use** Early dial-up connections were painfully slow. Graphics loaded line by line. Web pages were crude. Most people’s first experience involved cryptic commands, connection drops, and no clear purpose. It felt less like a revolution and more like a frustrating toy. **3. The “Why would anyone need this?” problem** Many couldn’t see a killer app beyond email. Critics compared it to CB radio or videophones—technologies that generated buzz but fizzled because they didn’t solve a compelling everyday problem. A 1995 Newsweek article famously declared: “The truth is no online database will replace your daily newspaper, no CD-ROM can take the place of a competent teacher and no computer network will change the way government works.” It imagined a future of reading on glowing screens and dismissed it as preposterous. **4. Comparisons to past hype cycles** The 1980s had seen the boom and bust of “interactive TV,” videotex systems (like France’s Minitel or Knight-Ridder’s Viewtron), and CD-ROM multimedia. People assumed the Internet was just the latest over-hyped technology destined to collapse under its own weight once the novelty wore off. **5. Business models were nonexistent** There was widespread confusion over how the Internet could make money. Navigation was free, content was often free, and advertising on the web was nearly unheard of before the mid-90s. Pundits thought commercial activity would be rejected by the “non-commercial” culture of the Net, or that security concerns (credit card theft) would kill e-commerce in its crib. **6. Cultural and psychological resistance** The Internet was often portrayed as a place for geeks, socially awkward loners, or shady characters. The media stoked fears of pornography, predators, and fake identities. Many saw it as an unhealthy substitute for real-life interaction—a passing subculture, not the future backbone of society. **7. Limited vision of its scale** Even supporters underestimated how deeply it would embed into daily life. People thought of it like a digital library, a shopping mall, or a new TV channel—not an infrastructure that would replace maps, travel agents, classified ads, banking, correspondence, and even social interaction. As Clifford Stoll wrote in 1995, “Computers and networks isolate us from one another.” **Reality check: Not everyone thought it was a fad** It’s worth noting that many in technology, academia, and early adopter circles were true believers. The Internet had been growing steadily since the 1980s, and once the Web made it graphical and point-and-click, mass adoption began its rocket rise. The skeptics were simply more vocal, and their arguments seemed plausible given the era’s limitations. The real shift happened around 1998–2000, when email, search engines, and e-commerce proved their utility—and then the dot-com bust briefly made the skeptics look prescient before the Internet proved them wrong for good.
